WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.870
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:09.940
A computer graphics paper from approximately
3 years ago was able to simulate the motion

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:15.050
of these bubbles, and even these beautiful
collision events between them in a matter

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:16.560
of milliseconds.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.859
This was approximately 300 episodes ago, and
in this series, we always say that two more

00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:25.110
papers down the line, and this will be improved
significantly.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:31.669
So now, once again, here goes another one
of those Two Minute Papers moments of truth.

00:00:31.669 --> 00:00:35.840
Now, three years later, let’s see how this
field evolved.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.180
Let’s fire up this new technique that will
now, simulate the evolution of two cube-shaped

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:42.460
droplets for us.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:47.600
In reality, mother nature would make sure
that the surface area of these droplets is

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:54.589
minimized, let’s see…yes, droplets form
immediately, so the simulation program understands

00:00:54.589 --> 00:00:59.860
surface tension, and the collision event is
also simulated beautifully.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.070
A+.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:07.100
However, this was possible with previous methods,
for instance, a paper by the name Surface-Only

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.970
Liquids could also pull it off, so what’s
new here?

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:14.370
Well, let’s look under the hood and find
out.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:15.830
Oh yes.

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.049
This is different.

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:22.420
You see, normally, if we do this breakdown,
we get triangle meshes, this is typically

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.210
how these surfaces are represented.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:28.450
But I don’t see any meshes here, I see particles!

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:32.150
Great, but what does this enable us to do?

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:33.150
Look here.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:37.939
If we break down the simulation of this beautiful
fluid polygon, we see that there is not only

00:01:37.939 --> 00:01:40.090
one kind of particle here!

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.200
There are three kinds!

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:48.539
With light blue, we see sheet particles, the
yellow ones are filament particles, and if

00:01:48.539 --> 00:01:54.000
we look inside, with dark blue here, you see
volume particles.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.030
With these building blocks, and the proposed
new simulation method, we can create much

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:01.890
more sophisticated surface tension-related
phenomena!

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.369
So let’s do exactly that!

00:02:04.369 --> 00:02:09.599
For instance, here, you see soap membranes
stretching due to wind flows.

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:17.230
They get separated, lots of topological changes
take place, and the algorithm handles it correctly.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:21.720
In an other example, this soap bubble has
been initialized with a hole, and you can

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:30.709
see it cascading through the entire surface.

00:02:30.709 --> 00:02:31.709
Beautiful work!

00:02:31.709 --> 00:02:36.480
And, after we finish the simulation of these
fluid chains, we can look under the hood,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.730
and see how the algorithm thinks about this
piece of fluid.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:45.790
Once again, with dark blue, we have the particles
that represent the inner volume of the water

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:50.550
chains, and on the outside, there is a thin
layer of sheet particles holding them together.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:53.210
What a clean and beautiful visualization.

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:57.270
So, how much do we have to wait to get these
results?

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:58.640
A bit.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.920
Simulating this fluid chain example took roughly
60 seconds per frame.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:09.280
This droplet on a plane example runs approximately
ten times faster than that, it needs only

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.739
6.5 seconds for each frame.

00:03:11.739 --> 00:03:15.940
This was one of the cheaper scenes in the
paper, and you may be wondering, which one

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:17.989
was the most expensive?

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:25.700
This water bell took almost two minutes for
each frame, and here, when you see this breakdown,

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:30.120
from the particle color coding, you know exactly
what we are looking at.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.700
Since part of this algorithm runs on your
processor, and a different part on your graphics

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:40.019
card, there is plenty of room for improvements
in terms of the computation time for a followup

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:41.019
paper.

00:03:41.019 --> 00:03:45.950
I cannot wait to see these beautiful simulations
in real time two more papers down the line.

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:47.370
What a time to be alive!

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:53.180
I also couldn’t resist creating a slow-motion
version of some of these videos, if this is

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:57.659
something that you wish to see, make sure
to click our Instagram page link in the video

00:03:57.659 --> 00:05:01.040
description

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:04.860
for more.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:08.530
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

